From: morgan@skyler.arc.ab.ca (Sean Morgan)
Date: Thu, 1 Dec 1994 16:57:19 -0700
Subject: [#94-12-56] singularity

"Peter C. McCluskey" <pcm@rahul.net> wrote:

> Here is something I submitted for the FAQ (a condensation of something
>Dani Eder posted to sci.nanotech)(arkuat, are you still working on the
>FAQ?):
> [ ... ]
>Q. When will the Singularity occur?
>
>The short answer is that the near edge of the Singularity is due about
>the year 2035 AD.  One indication is human population growth, which
>over the past 10,000 years has been following a hyperbolic trend.
>Since about 1600 AD the trend has been very steadily accelerating with
>the asymptote located in the year 2035 AD.  Now, either the human
>population will become infinite at that time, or a trend that has
>persisted over all of human history will be broken. Maybe it will
>become undefinable if we include uploaded humans. (That's just a
>backup copy. No! It's a clone...).

That claim caused quite the furor last time, since population growth should
be at most an exponential (or better yet, a sigmoid).  So he supplied his
data:

                Population
        Year    (billions)

           1       0.2
        1650       0.55
        1750       0.725
        1850       1.175
        1900       1.6
        1930       2
        1950       2.565
        1980       4.477
        1990       5.333

(Without worrying about significant figures) the best exponential growth
fit I can get is:
        Pop=1.201554165E-06 * exp( 7.583800136E-03 * Year)
(SSE 2.72467, R^2 = 0.89243), though I am not sure I have used the time
axis properly.


I get a much better fit (SSE = 0.06812, R^2 = 0.99731) with:
        Pop=204.120/(2027.63 - Year)
i.e. a hyperbolic (!)


The Singularity will occur on Aug.19, 2027 (plus or minus 1.83396 years, :-).

