Subject: [#94-12-58] BASICS:  Coalitions and firepower

Jay Reynolds Freeman writes:
>I suspect that there is also an issue of coordination and
>poorly-defined chain of command when small defense organizations
>attempt to amalgamate to meet a larger threat.  

Here is a thumbnail history of middle age Europe, as I recall it from
a teacher at a I.H.S.  week-long seminar.

After some particularly nasty behavior by rulers, a religious movement
gathered an army and forced European monarchs to sign a "Peace of God"
contract, which limited when they could go to war, and told the to
leave cities alone.  Amazingly, this army then disbanded and the kings
kept their word.  European cities then defended themselves, and kings
didn't get much involved in local affairs.

Then at some point kings began to conquer the cities one by one and
the cities didn't get their act together coordinating their defense
until it was too late.  So the problem was exactly the one Jay mentions.

Someone who knows history better can correct me.

Robin Hanson

