From: plaz@netcom.com (Plaz)
Date: Thu, 1 Dec 1994 17:03:17 -0800
Subject: [#94-12-59] BASICS: Anarchy & Roads

At  3:39 PM 12/1/94 -0800, Jordan Sparks wrote:
>I find this talk about private land and no gov't all very interesting but
>totally irrellevant to the present.

I disagree.

>In order for it to really work, we
>would need much better computers than we now have.  Just imagine how many
>people you would have to contact for permission to drive across town if
>there were no public land.  Also, before dealing with each individual,
>you would have to check her or his reputation.  Without faster computers,
>none of this is possible.  Give it thirty years, and I might accept its
>viability.  In the meantime, we must make do with the gov't.

This would not happen very often, in practice.

Market needs and profit opportunities would make setting up toll roads
popular. That and the requirement for shops and businesses to have patrons
have access to their stores would set up quite a network of roads.
Residential neighborhoods would probably have coalition owned streets, or
owned by the construction company that developed the neighborhood in the
first place. (This happens now, municipal government generally buys the
roads from the development company, eventually.) Road maintenance companies
would emerge.

So you see, you wouldn't really have to negotiate contracts with every
landowner involved in order to cross town. (Actually in practice, you would
be creating implicit contracts, but you probably wouldn't have to keep
track of every single one.

PS - I think you are underestimating existing computer technology. I think
it would be possible to set-up the system you describe today, complete with
digitally signed contracts. A good reputation market program hasn't been
set up yet, but I think that's an implimentation problem, not a computer
speed problem.

The set-up may not be *economical*, yet, but it would be possible.

