A couple comments now: while tree farms are bad, at least they
seem, AFAIK, to replant, unlike the government-subsidized tree
strip mining that goes on in the Tongass National Forest.

Also, I don't really recall anyone ever strip mining for oil.
Maybe you've been reading a little too much propaganda from
the probably coal-funded environmentalist movement.

(Prove it isn't so! Nuclear power has been regulated to thepoint
of nonviability, along with most of the oil industry; back when
oil was high in cost, we had price controls and a windfall profits
tax, all directed against domestic producers. The foreign producers
were all able to build war chests, while ours weren't. And while a
large amount of petroleum activity has caused pollution in the past,
this need not be the case. I don't really see how the same can be true
of a lot of coal strip mines, which seem to be virtually not affected
at all by the growing environemntal movement.)

(I'd also like to say that the oil exploration industry is probably
a very distant second as a source of past and current pollution in
this state, compared to the petrochemical and chemical industries,
but bears the brunt of environmental enforcement efforts, because
it simply doesn't have the money to pay off the politicians. Most
of the problem areas in the oil exploration's legacy were probably made
by companies that either are out of business or can't afford to clean
up the mess anyway due to the collapse of the industry; it seems logical
to me that an oil import fee would do a lot to strengthen both the
current industry's ability to deal with regulations while providing a fund
to clean up past errors, but in spite of all the trillions of dollars of
statist bull we have to put up with, this isn't done; meanwhile, the
strip mines go on...)

